.TH __CTPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__ctpool_destroy \- destroy a thread pool (internal)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

void __ctpool_destroy(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
pool is an opaque VALUE handle (a packed {slot index, generation}
pair), resolved through a library-owned slot table before the
underlying pool object is ever touched. pool must be a currently
live handle (one returned by create_cthread_pool/_mp and not yet
destroyed). CTPOOL_INVALID is a silent no-op. Any other value (a
stale handle already destroyed by an earlier call or concurrently
by another thread, a forged value, or garbage) is a FATAL ERROR:
this function calls fatal_err() (abort()/SIGABRT) rather than risk
a use-after-free or double-free.

On a live handle, waits for every in-flight resolved use of the
handle to finish (a ctpool_submit/_try_submit/_timed_submit/
_submit_future/_try_submit_future/_timed_submit_future/_wait/
_shutdown_drain/_shutdown_immediate/_pending_count/_active_count
call currently executing on another thread). If neither
ctpool_shutdown_drain nor ctpool_shutdown_immediate was called
beforehand, a drain shutdown runs first, BEFORE that wait: a
submitter blocked on a full bounded queue can depend entirely on
shutdown's own not_full broadcast to ever release its pin, so
waiting for quiescence before running shutdown would risk deadlock
against exactly the caller this function exists to protect. Frees
all associated resources once every pin has been released.

After this function returns the pool handle is invalid. Use the
ctpool_destroy macro rather than calling this function directly, as
the macro also sets the handle to CTPOOL_INVALID.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID is a no-op)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_destroy (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
